TITLE: Agentic AI and Large Language Models for Autonomous IoT Cybersecurity: A Systematic Survey, Taxonomy, and Research Roadmap
TEXT: Review Agentic AI and Large Language Models for Autonomous IoT Cybersecurity: A Systematic Survey, Taxonomy, and Research Roadmap Vinoth Nageshwaran 1,* and Soundararajan Ezekiel 2 1 School of Computer & Information Sciences, University of the Cumberlands, 6178 College Station Drive, Williamsburg, KY 40769, USA 2 Department of Mathematical and Computer Sciences, Indiana University of Pennsylvania, Indiana, PA 15705, USA; sezekiel@iup.edu * Correspondence: vnageshwaran41452@ucumberlands.edu Abstract Conventional signature-based defenses no longer protect the heterogeneous, large-scale infrastructures that the Internet of Things (IoT) now constitutes. Large language models (LLMs) and agentic artificial intelligence (AI)—systems that autonomously perceive, reason, plan, and act—open a path to self-defending IoT ecosystems, but the integrating literature remains fragmented. Within the IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, and MDPI literature, this survey is, to the best of our knowledge, among the first systematic reviews of agentic AI and LLM-driven approaches for autonomous IoT cybersecurity. Following a PRISMA 2020 protocol, we analyze 153 peer-reviewed studies published between 2020 and 2026 in IEEE Xplore, the ACM Digital Library, and MDPI journals. We organize the corpus along a four-pillar taxonomy: agent architecture (single- vs. multi-agent), reasoning strategy (chain-of-thought, ReAct, plan-and-solve, tool use), action scope (detection, response, threat hunting, vulnerability discovery, deception), and deployment topology (edge, fog, cloud). We synthesize four flagship application domains, consolidate datasets and benchmarks, and analyze open challenges including hallucination, prompt-injection robustness, explainability, privacy, latency, and governance. A 2026 research roadmap identifies federated agentic learning, verifiable autonomous reasoning, trustworthy multi-agent collaboration, and resource-hardened edge agents as high-priority directions. A companion reproducibility kit—prompt templates, reference single- and multi-agent loops, and an Edge-IIoTset-style evaluation harness, released as illustrative scaffolding rather than a validated framework—is released publicly and archived on Zenodo (DOI 10.5281/zenodo.20726552). Academic Editors: Andreas Mauthe and Nikolay Hinov Received: 29 May 2026 Revised: 17 June 2026 Accepted: 18 June 2026 Published: 22 June 2026 Copyright: © 2026 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license. Keywords: agentic AI; autonomous security; large language models; IoT cybersecurity; edge computing security; multi-agent systems; intrusion detection; prompt injection; federated learning; PRISMA; threat hunting; adversarial robustness 1. Introduction The global population of Internet of Things (IoT) endpoints is projected to exceed 30 billion devices by 2026, spanning consumer wearables, smart-city sensor fabrics, vehicular telematics units, medical implantables, and industrial control systems that supervise critical infrastructure [1–3]. The same heterogeneity, low-cost manufacturing economics, and decade-long deployment lifetimes that make IoT economically attractive also make it structurally hostile to traditional defensive postures. Device firmware is rarely patched, Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 2 of 42 cryptographic identities are inconsistent or absent, and the network traffic generated by tens of millions of microcontrollers cannot be triaged by human analysts at any plausible staffing ratio [2,4–6]. The Mozi botnet—which by 2021 had infected over 1.5 million IoT devices, established multiple large-scale peer-to-peer botnets, and generated more attack traffic than any contemporary IoT-malware family—exemplifies how rapidly modern IoTscale adversaries outpace conventional signature-based defense [7–9]. Subsequent Mirai variants and other IoT-botnet families continue to reproduce this pattern at scale [10–15]. Over the same period, two technological trajectories have matured in ways that promise a structural response to this asymmetry. The first is the emergence of large language models (LLMs) capable of zero-shot reasoning over heterogeneous textual artifacts—packet captures rendered as text, system logs, threat-intelligence reports, MITRE ATT&CK technique descriptions, CVE narratives—without bespoke feature engineering [16–21]. The second is the development of agentic AI: software systems that wrap LLM reasoning in perception, planning, memory, tool-use, and multi-agent communication loops, enabling them to autonomously execute multi-step security tasks that previously required human analysts [22–28]. The combination opens a credible path to self-defending IoT ecosystems in which heterogeneous traffic is interpreted semantically, anomalies are explained in natural language, response playbooks are dynamically synthesized, and adversarial actions are deceived in real time. Despite intense research activity, the literature integrating these two trajectories remains fragmented. Recent surveys treat LLMs in cybersecurity generally [20,21], generative AI in IoT broadly [1], or specific sub-domains such as SOC automation [27], CTI extraction [29–31], or autonomous penetration testing [32–36]. No prior systematic review unifies agent architecture, reasoning strategy, action scope, and deployment topology into a single taxonomy specifically for IoT cybersecurity, nor has any prior work mapped the field’s open problems onto a concrete 2026 research roadmap. The absence of such synthesis impedes both academic progress (because researchers cannot easily locate adjacent work) and industrial adoption (because practitioners cannot evaluate which agentic patterns are mature enough to deploy). 1.1. Scope and Contributions Within the IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, and MDPI literature, this is, to the best of our knowledge, among the first systematic surveys to unify agent architecture, reasoning strategy, action scope, and deployment topology into a single coordinate system specifically for IoT cybersecurity, to ground the synthesis in a 153-paper PRISMA-screened corpus restricted to IEEE Xplore, the ACM Digital Library, and MDPI journals from 2020 to 2026, and to release a companion open-source reproducibility kit (archived on Zenodo with DOI 10.5281/zenodo.20726552) that operationalizes the taxonomy. Our contributions are as follows: (1) A four-pillar taxonomy of agentic AI for IoT security, organized along agent architecture (single- vs. multi-agent), reasoning strategy (chain-of-thought, ReAct, planand-solve, tool-use), action scope (detection, response, threat hunting, vulnerability discovery, deception), and deployment topology (edge, fog, cloud). The taxonomy is operationalized in a companion open-source repository. (2) A critical synthesis of four flagship application domains—anomaly interpretation, intelligent response orchestration, predictive risk assessment, and adversarial deception—including quantitative comparisons of reported detection accuracy, mean-time-to-respond reductions, and edge inference latencies across studies. (3) A consolidated atlas of datasets and benchmarks for evaluating agentic IoT defenses, including CICIoT2023, Edge-IIoTset, TON_IoT, CICIoMT2024, and emergent LLM- https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 3 of 42 specific benchmarks such as CyberSecEval and CyberSOCEval. We critique the absence of agent-native evaluation suites that test multi-step tool use, latency budgets, and adversarial robustness simultaneously. (4) A rigorous analysis of open challenges, including hallucination under high-stakes decisioning, prompt-injection and tool-hijacking attacks on agents themselves, the latency-security paradox at the edge, privacy preservation under federated training, and the governance vacuum surrounding autonomous defensive action. (5) A 2026 research roadmap identifying four high-impact directions—federated agentic learning, verifiable autonomous reasoning, trustworthy multi-agent collaboration, and resource-hardened edge agents—with concrete experimental milestones. (6) A companion reproducibility kit (GitHub: https://github.com/vnageshwaran-de/ agentic-iot-security (accessed on 29 May 2026); Zenodo DOI 10.5281/zenodo.20726552, release v0.3.0, implemented in Python 3.11 [37]) comprising prompt templates for each action scope, reference single-agent ReAct and multi-agent coordination loops, and an evaluation harness for accuracy and per-decision latency on Edge-IIoTset-style traffic. The kit is positioned as scaffolding for follow-up empirical work rather than as a production framework—see Section 10 for explicit release status. We flag explicitly that the kit is an untested-in-production scaffolding artifact; this status is recorded as a limitation of the present work (Sections 3.8 and 10.5) rather than claimed as a strength. 1.2. Paper Organization Section 2 provides background on IoT security primitives, LLM architectures, and the agentic AI paradigm. Section 3 describes our PRISMA methodology and reports the corpus statistics. Section 4 situates this work against prior surveys. Section 5 presents the four-pillar taxonomy. Section 6 examines the four flagship application domains. Section 7 catalogs datasets and benchmarks. Section 8 analyzes open challenges. Section 9 articulates the 2026 research roadmap. Section 10 describes the companion code artifact. Section 11 concludes. 2. Background and Preliminaries 2.1. The IoT Security Landscape An IoT system is conventionally decomposed into three layers: a perception layer of resource-constrained sensors and actuators; a network layer comprising fog gateways, edge routers, 5G/6G slices, and wide-area protocols such as MQTT and CoAP; and an application layer of cloud analytics, dashboards, and orchestration platforms [1,2,38,39]. Each layer exhibits distinct attack surfaces: at the perception layer, firmware exploits and command injection [40,41]; at the network layer, traffic flooding, protocol fuzzing, and lateral movement [42–45]; at the application layer, credential abuse, supply chain compromise, and data exfiltration [46,47]. The 5G/6G transition compounds the problem by introducing network slicing—logical partitions whose isolation guarantees become themselves the target of slice-hopping attacks [38,39,48,49]. Defensive practice has historically relied on signature-based intrusion detection systems (IDS), supplemented by anomaly detectors based on autoencoders, isolation forests, and recurrent networks [2,50–52]. These approaches are brittle against zero-day attacks, exhibit high false-positive rates that drown out human analysts [53,54], and provide no native facility for explaining their decisions to operators [55–58]. Increasingly, researchers have turned to graph neural networks (GNNs) that exploit the topological structure of IoT traffic [59–64] and to federated learning that allows distributed devices to train shared anomaly detectors without exposing raw data [57,65,66]. Yet these methods, while improving raw accuracy, still operate as opaque pattern matchers; they cannot reason about novel attacker strategies, synthesize multi-step responses, or coordinate across heterogeneous tools. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 4 of 42 2.2. Large Language Models for Security Large language models are transformer-based neural networks trained on massive text corpora to predict the next token given a context. Three properties make them particularly suited to security applications. First, zero-shot generalization: a sufficiently large model can perform tasks for which it was not explicitly trained, including malware analysis, log triage, and threat-intelligence extraction [16,18–20]. Second, semantic interpretation of heterogeneous artifacts: an LLM can ingest a packet capture, a system log, and an attacker’s natural-language ransom note within the same context window and reason across them [67,68]. Third, natural-language explanation: an LLM can articulate why a decision was made in terms a human operator can audit [68–70]. Concrete results illustrate the potential. BARTPredict achieves 98% accuracy in predicting IoT attacks on the BoT-IoT benchmark (80/20 stratified train/test split as reported in the primary study) by fine-tuning a BART encoder on temporal traffic sequences [71]. Edgedeployed DistilBERT and TinyBERT models classify IoT intrusions at over 99% accuracy with sub-70-millisecond inference latency [72,73]. These headline figures are reported on different datasets (BoT-IoT and Edge-IIoTset, respectively) with different attack taxonomies, class distributions, and train/test protocols; they are therefore not statistically comparable with one another, and we return to this point—and to the 10–20 percentage-point crossdataset degradation that motivates it—in Section 7.1 (Table 1) and in the caveats attached to Table 2. LLM-augmented GPT-2 pipelines integrate signature-based detection with semantic reasoning to identify previously unseen attack variants [74]. APOLLO, a GPT4o phishing detector, generates both a classification and a human-readable explanation per email [75,76]. CyberNER-LLM extracts entities from threat-intelligence reports with accuracies that approach human-expert performance [29,31]. Table 1. Summary of cross-dataset performance degradation documented in the corpus, grouped by detector family. Bands aggregate the nine primary studies enumerated in the text; per-study figures appear in the coded extraction matrix shipped with the companion kit (Section 10). The table summarizes a recurring pattern—it is not a meta-analysis, because heterogeneous protocols, splits, and metrics prevent statistical pooling. Detector Family Representative Studies Transformer/LLM-based IoT detectors [2,3,65] Deep-learning IDS (autoencoder, CNN/RNN) [51,77] Botnet-focused detectors [14,15,78] Graph-based IDS [64] Typical In-Distribution Report >99% single-dataset accuracy 95–99% ∼99% on BoT-IoT/N-BaIoT High in-distribution F1 Cross-Dataset Behaviour 10–20 pp degradation on transfer; prompts and fine-tuning corpora entrench dataset-specific shortcuts 10–20 pp; sensitive to feature-distribution shift 10–20 pp; severe class imbalance additionally inflates in-distribution scores Degradation reported on second dataset; topology shift dominates Three families of efficiency techniques are critical for IoT deployment: quantization, which reduces model precision from 16- or 32-bit floats to 4- or 8-bit integers; pruning, which removes low-magnitude weights; and knowledge distillation, which trains a small “student” model to mimic a large “teacher” [79–85]. As reported by Wang et al. [72], DistilBERT retains approximately 97% of BERT-base accuracy on IoT-IDS tasks with around 40% fewer parameters and roughly 60% faster inference; cross-view distillation in IoT traffic analysis achieves 92–95% parameter reduction while sustaining macro-F1 above 0.986 [82]. These techniques are what enable LLMs to run on the constrained hardware that dominates the IoT installed base. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 5 of 42 Table 2. Reported accuracy–latency–robustness frontier across deployment tiers. Latencies are p50 inference-plus-tool-call times; accuracies are best-reported numbers on the named benchmarks; prompt-injection numbers reference CyberSecEval baselines [19,86] when the primary study did not measure them directly. Cells in italics marked with an asterisk (*) denote extrapolated or unmeasured values; all other cells are direct measurements from the cited primary literature. Important caveat: accuracy figures are best-reported peak values on the named benchmark dataset (parenthetical citation); the rows are not directly comparable because the benchmarks (Edge-IIoTset, CICIoT2023, others) differ in attack distributions, class balance, and difficulty (see Section 7.1 on the 10–20 percentage-point cross-dataset degradation we document for this corpus). Latency figures pool across heterogeneous hardware platforms (Raspberry Pi-class, Cortex-A78-class, x86 gateway, datacenter GPU) and measurement conventions (inference-only vs. end-to-end including tool calls); readers should consult the cited primary studies for the specific hardware and measurement protocol of each reported value. The “Benchmark scale/difficulty” column makes non-comparability explicit: because the per-row benchmarks differ in class count, device heterogeneity, and class balance, accuracy cells must not be compared across rows, and no ranking of tiers by accuracy is implied. Reading rule (Section 7.1): all accuracy cells here are indicative of trends only; only within-study/within-dataset deltas—such as the transfer drops in Table 1—qualify as directly comparable. Tier Edge/on-device Fog/gateway Cloud-centric Model Class Distilled BERT, LLaMA-3.2-1B, SLM (4-bit) 7–13 B params (Mistral, Llama-3-8B) 70B+ params, frontier API models p50 Latency Best-Reported acc. (Dataset) 70–400 ms ~99% on EdgeIIoTset [72,73] 300–900 ms (incl. 1 tool call) ~98% on CICIoT2023 [16,74,87] 1–5 s end-to-end ~98% prediction acc. [71]; >90% MA-IDS [23] Benchmark Scale/Difficulty Edge-IIoTset: 14 attack classes; IIoT-protocol-centric; comparatively small (Section 7.1)—favourable to high absolute accuracy CICIoT2023: 33 attacks/7 families on 100+ real devices—larger and more heterogeneous than row 1’s benchmark BoT-IoT and mixed benchmarks: severe class imbalance (benign share <1% in BoT-IoT) inflates accuracy-style metrics Prompt-Injection Susceptibility Largely unmeasured *; CyberSecEval baseline 26–41% [19,86] 25–40% on production probes * 10–30% with polymorphic- prompt defense [88] 2.3. The Agentic AI Paradigm Agentic AI refers to systems that augment LLM reasoning with four additional capabilities: (i) perception, the ability to observe state via sensors, APIs, or document retrieval; (ii) planning, the ability to decompose a goal into ordered sub-tasks; (iii) tool use, the ability to invoke external functions such as SIEM queries, packet sniffers, or sandbox executions; and (iv) memory, the ability to retain information across interactions [22,26,86]. These capabilities are coordinated by control loops, the most influential of which are ReAct (Yao S. et al., ICLR 2023 [89]), which interleaves natural-language reasoning with tool invocations [25]; plan-and-solve, which produces a complete plan before execution; and reflection, which critiques prior outputs before committing [28,32]. Single-agent designs concentrate all four capabilities in one LLM-driven loop. Multiagent designs distribute them across specialized agents—for example, a scanner agent, an exploit-selection agent, and a post-exploitation agent in autonomous penetration testing [36]—that communicate via natural language, structured messages, or shared blackboards. Multi-agent systems exhibit emergent properties: debate among phishingclassification agents reduces false negatives [90]; writer-reviewer loops for automotive threat analysis produce richer attack trees [91]; cooperative threat-detection agents reach over 90% classification accuracy on multi-attack IoT benchmarks [23,92]. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 6 of 42 The agentic paradigm is not without cost. Agents inherit and amplify the weaknesses of the underlying LLM—hallucination, prompt sensitivity, and contextual drift—and propagate them across longer decision chains [20,21]. They introduce new attack surfaces: prompt injection via untrusted observations [88], tool hijacking, and emergent collusion in multiagent settings [22,26]. And they raise governance questions about accountability when autonomous decisions cause harm [27,28]. The remainder of this paper systematically examines how these costs and capabilities play out specifically in the IoT cybersecurity domain. 3. Methodology: A PRISMA-Style Systematic Review 3.1. Research Questions The synthesis is structured around five research questions (RQs) derived from the gap analysis in Section 1: (RQ1) Which agent architectures and reasoning strategies dominate the published IoT-cybersecurity literature, and how do they trade off coordination overhead against detection accuracy? (RQ2) What detection-accuracy versus end-to-end latency frontier has been reported for LLM-driven IoT defenders at the edge, fog, and cloud tiers? (RQ3) How do existing designs mitigate LLM-specific adversarial risks (prompt injection, hallucination, tool hijacking), and what residual exposure remains? (RQ4) Which datasets and benchmarks are used to validate agentic IoT defenders, and what aspects of agent behavior (multi-step tool use, latency, robustness) remain unmeasured? (RQ5) Which research directions, if pursued in the next 24 months, would most materially close the gap between laboratory demonstrations and trustworthy production deployments? Each subsequent section returns to these RQs explicitly. 3.2. Search Strategy We conducted a systematic literature search across three peer-reviewed venues—IEEE Xplore, the ACM Digital Library, and MDPI journals—covering publications from January 2020 through May 2026. The final search was conducted on 29 May 2026. The temporal lower bound was chosen to coincide with the publication of GPT-3 and the subsequent acceleration of LLM-based security research; the upper bound reflects the most recent indexed literature at the time of writing. We deliberately excluded preprint repositories (including arXiv) to ensure that every cited study had passed formal peer review. We disclose that the present manuscript is itself being submitted to an MDPI journal (Electronics); 56 of the 153 included studies (37%) come from MDPI venues. Readers should weight the corpus accordingly, and we discuss the likely effect of the omitted venues in Section 3.8. We note that ACM Digital Library coverage is interpreted to include peer-reviewed works cross-listed from publisher partners (e.g., select Elsevier and Springer journals indexed and accessible through ACM DL); such cross-listed entries remain within scope because they meet the peer-review and discoverability criteria of our three-venue policy. Search strings were composed by intersecting two term groups. The first group captured the AI methodology of interest: “large language model” OR “LLM” OR “agentic AI” OR “multi-agent” OR “ReAct” OR “chain-of-thought” OR “tool use”. The second group captured the IoT cybersecurity application: “IoT” OR “Internet of Things” OR “IIoT” OR “edge security” OR “intrusion detection” OR “threat detection” OR “anomaly detection” OR “vulnerability discovery” OR “honeypot” OR “SOAR”. We also ran targeted queries for foundational topics that anchor the survey, including datasets (“Edge-IIoTset”, “CICIoT2023”, “TON_IoT”, “CICIoMT2024”), efficiency techniques (“quantization”, “distillation”, “pruning”, “small language model”), and emerging concerns (“prompt injection”, “hallucination”, “federated learning”). https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 7 of 42 3.3. Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria Studies were included if they (a) were peer-reviewed publications indexed in IEEE Xplore, ACM DL, or MDPI; (b) appeared between January 2020 and May 2026; (c) addressed either the use of LLMs/agentic AI for IoT cybersecurity, or the security of LLM/agentic systems themselves in IoT contexts, or foundational techniques (datasets, efficiency methods, evaluation benchmarks) on which the field demonstrably builds. Studies were excluded if they (a) addressed AI techniques unrelated to language models or agents (pure CNN/RNN intrusion detection from before 2022 was retained only where it served as a foundational baseline); (b) lacked an empirical evaluation; (c) were duplicate publications, errata, or editorial commentary. 3.4. Screening Procedure and PRISMA Flow Title-and-abstract screening and subsequent full-text screening were conducted by the first author against the inclusion criteria of Section 3.3. The senior author independently rescreened a 15% random sample at each stage to spot-check decisions; disagreements were resolved by discussion and resulted in adjustments to seven inclusion decisions across the 73 papers in the spot-check sample (Cohen’s kappa = 0.84, percentage agreement = 90.4%, computed against the binary include/exclude decisions). One paper was additionally reclassified as out-of-window during a full-text re-screen after the initial title-and-abstract pass, consistent with the “1 of the papers was reclassified out-of-window” note in the PRISMA flow diagram (Figure 1). We did not pre-register the review protocol on PROSPERO because the platform’s scope is biomedical; instead, the search strings, screening criteria, and exclusion rationale are released alongside the corpus in the companion code repository so that any reader can replicate the corpus selection. The initial searches yielded 487 candidate records: 198 from IEEE Xplore, 142 from ACM DL, and 147 from MDPI. After removing 63 duplicates (papers indexed in more than one venue or appearing as both conference and journal extensions of the same work), 424 records remained for title-and-abstract screening. Of these, 218 were excluded for being out of scope (e.g., AI for non-IoT cybersecurity with no transferable IoT relevance, or IoT papers using neither LLMs nor agents). The remaining 206 papers underwent full-text screening, during which 53 additional records were excluded—31 for insufficient empirical validation, 14 with fewer than 2 pages of substantive content, 7 for being non-English language without translation, and 1 for falling outside the 2020 temporal lower bound after author-date reverification. A final corpus of 153 studies entered the synthesis. Figure 1 reproduces this flow as a PRISMA diagram. For auditability, we make the screening and coding protocol explicit. Each of the seven extraction dimensions defined in Section 3.5 was governed by a closed codebook of mutually exclusive category definitions and an associated tie-breaking rule; the codebook is released as corpus/codebook.md alongside the coded matrix so that the rules can be inspected and re-applied. Coding proceeded in two passes: a first-author pass that assigned every dimension of every included study from the codebook, and a senior-author re-coding of the 15% spot-check sample. We distinguished and resolved two classes of disagreement separately. (a) Include/exclude disagreements were resolved by discussion to consensus and produced the seven adjusted inclusion decisions reported above (Cohen’s κ = 0.84, 90.4% raw agreement on the binary decision). (b) Dimension-coding disagreements on alreadyincluded papers were resolved by re-reading the primary study against the codebook rule and, where the study did not disclose a value, coding the dimension as not reported rather than inferring one—a conservative rule that prevents the synthesis from overstating what the literature measured. The residual disagreements concentrated on the reasoning-strategy dimension, where papers that do not name their control loop blur https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 8 of 42 the ReAct/plan-and-solve boundary; we now resolve that specific boundary with the explicit decision rules added to Sections 5 and 5.2. We did not compute a corpus-wide multi-rater coefficient because second-coder coverage was confined to the spot-check sample, and we record this as a residual limitation (Section 3.8); to support independent inspection, the coding rules and the full coded matrix are released as corpus/codebook.md and corpus/extraction_matrix.csv (the latter tagging each row by coding provenance), so that the corpus selection and coding decisions can be replicated. Figure 1. PRISMA 2020 flow for the systematic review (2020–2026, IEEE/ACM/MDPI). The diagram follows the standard PRISMA convention: arrows trace the main study flow down the page, the red boxes denote records excluded at each stage (with the reason and count), and the green box denotes the studies retained in the final synthesis. The symbol “§” refers to a section of this article. 3.5. Data Extraction Schema Each included study was coded along seven dimensions: (1) study identifier—title, primary author, venue, year; (2) IoT layer/topology—perception, network, application, edge, fog, cloud; (3) agent architecture—single-agent, multi-agent, or non-agentic baseline; (4) reasoning strategy—chain-of-thought, ReAct, plan-and-solve, tool-use, none; (5) cybersecurity focus—detection, response, threat hunting, vulnerability discovery, deception, authentication, threat intelligence; (6) key benchmarks—dataset and metric used; and (7) critical limitations—latency, hallucination, dataset bias, adversarial fragility, scalability. 3.6. Corpus Distribution The 153 included studies are distributed across the search window as follows: 9 in 2020, 14 in 2021, 18 in 2022, 24 in 2023, 36 in 2024, 38 in 2025, and 14 in the first five months of 2026 (the cut-off month for this review). By venue, the corpus comprises 58 papers from IEEE Xplore, 39 from the ACM Digital Library, and 56 from MDPI journals. By IoT sub-domain, 71 papers address general IoT or consumer IoT, 22 industrial IoT, 11 medical IoT, 9 vehicular IoT (IoV), 6 smart-grid IoT, and 35 are cross-cutting works on LLM/agent https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 9 of 42 capabilities applicable to multiple IoT settings. The temporal skew toward 2024–2025 reflects the acceleration of agentic-AI research after the public release of capable functioncalling models in late 2023; we explicitly mark this skew as a limitation in Section 3.7. 3.7. PRISMA-Corpus vs. Background References For methodological transparency, we distinguish the 153-paper PRISMA corpus (i.e., the studies that entered the systematic synthesis after the screening described in Sections 3.3 and 3.4) from a separate set of background references that the manuscript also cites. Background references are not screened against the PRISMA inclusion criteria; they exist to anchor the manuscript in well-established foundational work that predates or sits outside the systematic-review scope. They include the canonical methodology papers cited in Section 5.2.1 (CoT), Section 5.2.2 (ReAct), Section 8.2 (Greshake on indirect prompt injection; Carlini on adversarial alignment), Section 9.1 (McMahan on FedAvg), the regulatory and governance documents cited in Section 8.6 (NIST AI RMF, EU AI Act, ENISA), and the Mozi-botnet primary studies [7–9] cited in Section 1 (these are introduced as background context for the IoT threat landscape rather than as objects of the PRISMA synthesis, and are therefore listed under background references; the rationale is that they predate or sit outside the agentic-AI/LLM scope of the inclusion criteria in Section 3.3). These citations appear with reference numbers [6–9,89,93–98], together with a self-citation of the companion reproducibility kit at [37], and do not contribute to the 153-paper PRISMA corpus count. 3.8. Limitations of This Review Six limitations are disclosed in the interest of methodological transparency. First, the corpus is restricted to three venues; high-quality work in journals such as Computer Networks (Elsevier), Computers and Security (Elsevier), Journal of Network and Computer Applications (Elsevier), Journal of Cybersecurity (Springer), and venues such as USENIX Security, IEEE S&P, and NDSS would have been included by a broader policy but lies outside the protocol agreed for this review. We expect the most likely effect of these omissions to be: (i) under-representation of systems-security work on firmware analysis and protocol fuzzing (more common in USENIX/S&P/NDSS than in our three venues); (ii) under-representation of empirical-evaluation papers on large-scale IoT botnets and DDoS observation studies (more common in Elsevier Computer Networks); and (iii) some under-representation of formal-methods and verification work on agent reasoning (occasionally in Springer journals). We do not expect the four-pillar taxonomy itself to shift materially under a broadened search; we do expect the cataloged counts in Sections 5 and 6 to grow, particularly in the detection and threat-hunting scopes. Future revisions of this synthesis should broaden the venue policy accordingly. In direct response to reviewer feedback, and to improve representativeness without altering the reproducible corpus counts, we now add a complementary narrative scan of representative work from USENIX Security, IEEE S&P, ACM CCS, and NDSS in Section Representative Work from Top-Tier Security Venues, locating each contribution on the four-pillar coordinate system; that scan is explicitly outside the systematic protocol and does not contribute to the 153-paper count. To partially mitigate the venue-restriction bias rather than merely disclose it, we examined the most directly comparable systematic reviews published in the excluded venues. Zhang et al. [99], in Springer’s Cybersecurity, review over 300 works on LLMs across cybersecurity tasks and independently identify hallucination, prompt-injection exposure, and evaluation immaturity as the dominant open problems—consistent with Section 8 of this survey. Jamshidi et al. [100], in Elsevier’s Internet of Things, systematically review LLM applications to IoT security and privacy through 2025 and likewise report that cross-dataset https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 10 of 42 validation is rare and that production evidence lags laboratory results. The convergence of these excluded-venue syntheses with ours increases our confidence that the four-pillar taxonomy and the open-challenge analysis are not artifacts of the three-venue restriction; conversely, neither work centers multi-step agentic control loops, deployment topology, or a reproducibility artifact, so the positioning of Table 3 is unchanged. Second, screening was performed primarily by one reviewer with a 15% spot-check by the senior author rather than by two fully independent reviewers; inter-reviewer agreement was measured on the 15% spot-check sample (Cohen’s kappa = 0.84; see Section 3.4) rather than computed across the full corpus by two independent reviewers. Third, for paywalled IEEE Xplore and ACM Digital Library papers where institutional access did not include the full text, extraction was based on the publicly available abstract, title, and indexed metadata—a standard practice for systematic surveys but one that constrains the depth of methodological assessment for those papers. Fourth, the temporal upper bound is May 2026; papers published after that date are excluded. Fifth, the corpus is English-only; non-English peer-reviewed contributions to the field are not represented. Sixth, the companion reproducibility kit released with this survey is a v0.3.0 scaffolding artifact: it has not been validated in any production IoT environment, its only shipped validation run uses a deterministic stub on synthetic traffic (Appendix A), and any statement in this survey that references the kit should therefore be read as a proposal whose empirical force depends on the staged real-data validation path set out in Section 10.3, not as established capability. Table 3. Positioning of this survey against directly comparable prior work within the IEEE Xplore, ACM DL, and MDPI corpus. Direct comparator surveys published in venues outside this scope (e.g., recent LLM-for-security surveys in journals such as Sensors and other Elsevier/Springer outlets) are not represented; their omission is discussed in Section 3.8. The “This work” code-artifact cell is abbreviated; the full Zenodo DOI is 10.5281/zenodo.20726552 (release v0.3.0). Criteria for cell values: “LLM-agent coverage”—Yes = at least one section dedicated to multi-step LLM agents with tool use; Partial = LLMs discussed but agentic loop not centered; No = LLMs not the AI subject. “IoT focus”—Yes (core) = IoT is the primary scope; Yes = IoT scope is one of several; Subsection = IoT covered in a single subsection; Enterprise = enterprise networks rather than IoT; LLM-IoT = LLM-IoT integration specifically. “Edge topology”—Yes = explicit treatment of edge/fog/cloud trade-offs with quantitative latency or resource figures; Partial = edge mentioned with figures for a subset of tiers; Mentioned = edge named without quantitative resource analysis; No = absent. “Code artifact”—Yes = referenced repository with prompts, loops, and evaluation; No = no companion artifact. † Companion kit publicly available at https://github.com/vnageshwaran-de/agentic-iot-security (accessed on 29 May 2026) and archived on Zenodo with DOI 10.5281/zenodo.20726552 (release v0.3.0); positioned as scaffolding rather than a production framework—see Section 10.5 [37] for explicit release status. Survey Yao et al. [20] Ferrag et al. [1] Ali et al. [101] Tariq et al. [27] Bansal et al. [86] This work Year 2025 2024 2026 2025 2025 2026 LLM-Agent Coverage Partial No (GenAI) Yes Yes Yes (eval) Yes (deep) IoT Focus Subsection Yes LLM-IoT Enterprise No Yes (core) Edge Topology No Partial Mentioned No No Yes Code Artifact No No No No No Yes (Zenodo)† 4. Related Surveys and Positioning Several recent surveys touch on adjacent territory but stop short of the agentic AI × IoT cybersecurity intersection that this paper synthesizes. Yao et al. [20] survey LLM applications across eight cybersecurity domains, including a brief subsection on IoT, but treat agents only tangentially. Ferrag et al. [1] provide a comprehensive review of generative AI in IoT security, with strong coverage of GANs and diffusion models but limited treatment of multi-agent reasoning. The cognitive-layer survey of Ali et al. [101] explicitly addresses LLM-IoT integration but is structured around defense techniques rather than agent archi- https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 11 of 42 tectures. AI-Augmented SOC surveys [27,54] cover agent-based SOC automation in detail but are oriented toward enterprise networks rather than IoT edge constraints. Bansal et al.’s evaluation-benchmarking survey of LLM agents [86] catalogs evaluation methodology but does not address IoT-specific deployment concerns. Domain-specific surveys are similarly partial. IoV security has been reviewed by Maoudi et al. [102] and Junejo et al. [103] without an LLM agent lens. SOAR and threathunting surveys [27,28] focus on workflow automation rather than autonomous reasoning. The XAI-for-IoT survey of Ahmed et al. [104] examines explainability for federated IoT but predates agent-native explanation. Reviews on deep reinforcement learning for IoT intrusion detection [105] and graph-based IoT IDS [62,63] address complementary AI paradigms without LLM integration. Table 3 summarizes the positioning of this survey against the most directly comparable prior work. Compared to surveys that cover LLMs in cybersecurity broadly [20,27], we restrict scope to IoT but go deeper on agent architecture and edge deployment. Compared to IoT-security surveys that cover all AI techniques [1,2], we restrict the AI lens to agentic systems but extend coverage to 2026 publications and consolidate a companion code artifact. No prior survey in the IEEE Xplore, ACM DL, or MDPI corpora indexes a fourpillar taxonomy (architecture × reasoning × scope × topology) of the form developed here. The closest excluded-venue comparators—the Springer and Elsevier systematic reviews discussed in Section 3.8 [99,100]—are likewise partial with respect to this intersection: both treat LLMs-for-security broadly, but neither centers agentic control loops, IoT deployment topology, or a companion reproducibility artifact. Representative Work from Top-Tier Security Venues The three-venue protocol of Section 3.2 deliberately omits the flagship systems-security venues—USENIX Security, IEEE S&P, ACM CCS, and NDSS—where much of the foundational LLM- and agent-security work appears. Re-running the PRISMA search over those venues would change the reproducible corpus counts and is deferred to a future revision (Section 3.8); here we instead provide a complementary narrative scan of representative work from those venues and locate each contribution on the four-pillar coordinate system, so that the reader can see both what the protocol omits and why the taxonomy is robust to the omission. This scan is explicitly outside the systematic protocol: the works below were selected by expert judgement rather than the reproducible screening of Section 3.4, and they do not contribute to the 153-paper count. On the agentic-architecture axis, PentestGPT (Deng et al., USENIX Security 2024 [106]) decomposes automated penetration testing into self-interacting reasoning, generation, and parsing modules—a multi-agent, ReAct-style, cloud-topology system in our taxonomy—and corroborates the coordination-overhead-versus-capability trade-off cataloged in Section 5.1.2. On vulnerability discovery, KARONTE (Redini et al., IEEE S&P 2020 [107]) and ChatAFL (Meng et al., NDSS 2024 [108]) represent the systems-security firmware- and protocolfuzzing tradition we flagged as under-represented: KARONTE is a non-LLM multi-binary taint-tracking baseline, whereas ChatAFL uses an LLM to extract machine-readable protocol grammars for structure-aware mutation, directly extending the semantic-input-generation capability we attribute to LLM-driven fuzzers in Section 5.3.4 and the MQTT/CoAP fuzzing line of [109]. On adversarial robustness, the USENIX Security 2024 prompt-injection benchmark of Liu et al. [110] formalizes injection attacks and defenses and supplies exactly the standardized susceptibility measurement our Section 8.2 argues the IoT corpus lacks, while “Demystifying RCE Vulnerabilities in LLM-Integrated Apps” (Liu et al., ACM CCS 2024 [111]) shows that prompt injection can escalate to remote code execution in toolaugmented applications—the concrete realization of the tool-hijacking surface our Table 4 https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 12 of 42 layers defenses against. Finally, on evaluation methodology, the widely cited “Dos and Don’ts of Machine Learning in Computer Security” (Arp et al., USENIX Security 2022 [112]) enumerates pitfalls—notably inflated accuracy under unrealistic class balance and sampling bias—that independently motivate the cross-dataset-degradation and comparability cautions we raise in Sections 7.1 and 7.3. Table 4. Layered mitigation strategies for prompt injection and multi-agent vulnerabilities, consolidated from the surveyed corpus. Layers are ordered from input-adjacent to procedural; each layer’s residual exposure motivates the next. Kit pointers refer to the companion repository (Section 10.3). Defense Layer 1. Syntactic input sanitization 2. Untrusted-input framing/polymorphic prompts 3. Tool-call gating, allow-lists, dependency graphs 4. Inter-agent boundary isolation and per-hand-off sanitization 5. Human-approval gates for consequential actions 6. Continuous adversarial benchmarking Attack Surface Addressed Control-sequence smuggling in observations (indirect injection) Direct and indirect prompt injection Tool hijacking; foot-in-the-door escalation Context poisoning across multi-agent hand-offs Residual autonomous-action risk Measurement and regression detection Residual Exposure Semantically valid injections pass filters Framing erosion over long contexts Abuse of legitimately allowed tools Collusion and emergent coordination failures Alert fatigue; approval rubber-stamping Benchmark-to-deployment distribution shift Representative Refs/Kit Artifact [94]; kit sanitize.py, stage 1 [88]; kit sanitize.py, stage 2 [22,26] [26]; kit multi-agent coordinator [27,28] [19,86]; kit probe corpus Two conclusions follow. First, every one of these works maps cleanly onto an existing four-pillar coordinate, supporting our claim (Section 3.8) that the taxonomy does not shift under a broadened venue policy; what shifts is the population density of certain cells, especially vulnerability discovery and detection. Second, the convergence of these independent, top-tier results with our open-challenge analysis—prompt-injection exposure, evaluation immaturity, and the firmware/protocol-fuzzing gap—strengthens rather than undermines the synthesis. We therefore retain the three-venue protocol for the systematic counts while incorporating this cross-venue discussion to improve representativeness. 5. A Four-Pillar Taxonomy of Agentic AI for IoT Cybersecurity We organize the surveyed literature along four orthogonal dimensions. Together they yield a 4-tuple coordinate that locates every study and surfaces the under-explored regions of the design space. Figure 2 renders the taxonomy as a hierarchy tree. Because the four pillars are analytic projections of a single system rather than physically disjoint partitions, a study can appear to touch several categories at once. We therefore define category membership by primary-function decision rules rather than by surface features, and apply the following four rules consistently (they are the operative content of the codebook referenced in Section 3.4). (i) Architecture (single- vs. multi- vs. hybrid/hierarchical) is decided by the number of independently-prompted LLM control loops, not by the number of tools: a single LLM that calls many tools remains single-agent, whereas a manager LLM that delegates to separately-prompted worker LLMs is hierarchical even when it runs on one host. (ii) Action scope is coded by the most consequential action the agent is authorized to take, not by everything it computes: an agent that both classifies traffic and opens a containment ticket is coded response, not detection, because response carries the higher blast radius—which is precisely why detection-only is the conservative floor of Section 5.3.1. (iii) Reasoning strategy is coded from the control loop the paper actually implements—interleaved reasoning-and-tool-calls is ReAct, a complete plan committed before acting is plan-and-solve, and neither is coded none/CoT; the ambiguous middle https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 13 of 42 Action Scope Detection Response Threat hunting Vulnerability discovery Deception (an unnamed loop with a single mid-stream tool call) is resolved in favor of ReAct and flagged. (iv) Deployment topology is coded by where the LLM inference physically executes, so a hybrid edge-ML/cloud-LLM detector is coded cloud for its LLM component while its hybrid character is recorded under the architecture pillar. Worked illustrations of the genuinely overlapping cases: HuntGPT [18] = (single-agent, CoT, detection, cloud); CurriculumPT [36] = (multi-agent, plan-and-solve, vulnerability discovery, cloud); the SEED edge IDS [73] = (single-agent, none, detection, edge); and the hybrid LLM-IDS designs of [16,74,87] = (hybrid architecture, ReAct, detection-then-response, fog+cloud). Each overlapping study is thus assigned a deterministic primary coordinate while its secondary membership is retained in the extraction matrix, which is why the counts in Table 5 do not double-count. Table 5. Distribution of surveyed studies across action scopes and agent architectures. Non-agentic baselines (GNN-IDS, isolation forest, autoencoder-based IDS) are not tabulated here; they appear as comparators in the cited primary studies and are discussed in Section 6 where directly relevant. Single-Agent Studies [16,17,71–74,85,113,114] [115–117] [24,25,118] [40,109,119–124] [126,127] Multi-Agent Studies [23,87,92] [28] [87] [32,33,35,36,125] [128] Representative Deployment Edge SLM + cloud LLM fallback Fog gateway + SOC LLM Cloud LLM with SOC tools Cloud heavy LLM + sandbox tools Fog/gateway honeypot Figure 2. The four-pillar taxonomy of agentic AI for IoT cybersecurity. The worked examples in the figure’s legend correspond to BARTPredict [71], CurriculumPT [36], and the SEED edge IDS [73]; the corresponding reference numbers should be read from these caption citations. The symbol “§” refers to a section of this article. 5.1. Pillar I—Agent Architecture Agent architecture concerns the number and topology of LLM-driven control loops involved in a defensive task. We distinguish three classes. 5.1.1. Single-Agent Systems A single LLM, possibly equipped with tools and memory, executes the entire defensive workflow. This pattern dominates early LLM-for-security work and remains the natural choice for narrow tasks. BARTPredict [71] fine-tunes a single BART encoder to predict IoT attacks; HuntGPT [18] combines a single GPT-3.5 instance with a feature-importance https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 14 of 42 module for SOC triage; iThelma [25] operates as a single autonomous threat-hunting agent that consumes Splunk SOC playbooks. Single-agent designs minimize coordination overhead and are easier to deploy at the edge but cannot decompose complex tasks across role-specialized reasoners. 5.1.2. Multi-Agent Systems Multi-agent designs distribute the workflow across specialized agents that communicate via natural language, structured messages, or shared memory. Three coordination patterns recur. (i) Pipeline coordination: agents are arranged as a directed acyclic graph through which artifacts flow, exemplified by CurriculumPT’s pentest stages [36] and PentestAgent’s reconnaissance-exploit-post-exploit chain [33]. (ii) Debate coordination: agents argue from different positions to surface false negatives, as in debate-driven phishing detection [90] and the writer-reviewer automotive threat analysis loop [91]. (iii) Blackboard coordination: agents read and write to a shared state, as in the multi-agent IoT threat detection framework of Chen et al. [23,92] and Audit-LLM’s three-agent log analysis pipeline (Decomposer, Tool Builder, Executor) cited in [27]. Multi-agent systems consistently outperform single-agent baselines on benchmarks where reasoning must traverse multiple modalities or tools. The collaborative IoT intrusion detection framework of Liu et al. [23] reports over 90% classification accuracy across three benchmark datasets, attributable to specialization gains. However, multi-agent systems amplify cost (more LLM calls per task), latency (sequential coordination), and attack surface (each inter-agent message is a prompt-injection vector [26,88]). These amplification pathways are not merely theoretical: Section 8.2 catalogs the compound failure modes—error propagation, context poisoning, and tool-use amplification—that arise specifically from the multi-agent hand-offs described here, and quantifies how rarely the surveyed literature evaluates them. Interoperability across multi-agent systems is presently underspecified in the surveyed IoT-security literature. Outside the IoT domain, three protocol proposals are converging on a de facto interface (per our reading of the protocol specifications and recent deployment reports; this convergence claim is the authors’ synthesis and not a finding established in any single primary study) for agent-to-agent communication: the Model Context Protocol (MCP) for exposing tools and resources, the Agent Network Protocol (ANP) for agent-toagent discovery and capability negotiation, and the Agent-to-Agent (A2A) message schema for cross-vendor task hand-off. None of the IoT-security multi-agent systems in our corpus explicitly conforms to any of these proposals, an observation we return to as a roadmap item in Section 9.3. 5.1.3. Hybrid and Hierarchical Designs Hierarchical agents place a high-level “manager” LLM that decomposes goals and delegates to specialized sub-agents, which themselves may invoke tools or further agents. SOAR-LLM [28], Agentic AI for threat hunting [24,25], and the SOC hyper-automation architecture of Alzughaibi et al. [28] follow this pattern. Hybrid designs combine deterministic ML detectors (which run at line rate at the edge) with LLM agents (which reason on flagged events from the fog or cloud), exemplified by the SEED edge-cloud architecture and several hybrid LLM-IDS designs [16,74,87]. Hybrids appear to be the most plausible deployment pattern for production IoT environments because they amortize LLM cost across only the events that require semantic reasoning. 5.2. Pillar II—Reasoning Strategy Reasoning strategy concerns the structure of the LLM’s internal deliberation. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 15 of 42 5.2.1. Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting, introduced by Wei et al. at NeurIPS 2022 [93], elicits the model to generate intermediate reasoning steps before committing to an answer. In IoT log analysis, CoT prompting raises spam-detection accuracy to 0.96 without taskspecific training, and CoT+RAG combinations integrated with on-device LLaMA 3.2 and Gemma 3 enable edge anomaly reasoning [70]. CoT is the lowest-coordination strategy and is suitable for narrow classification tasks, but it remains vulnerable to logical leaps in high-stakes scenarios and to “reasoning hallucination” where intermediate steps drift from the evidence [21,70]. 5.2.2. ReAct and Tool-Augmented Reasoning ReAct—introduced by Yao S. et al. at ICLR 2023 [89]—interleaves natural-language reasoning steps with tool invocations, enabling the agent to query SIEMs, run packet captures, consult MITRE ATT&CK, or trigger sandbox executions in the middle of a deliberation [24,25,89]. ReAct is the dominant pattern for threat-hunting agents because hunting is fundamentally a ReAct workload—it requires interleaved external evidence gathering. However, ReAct agents are uniquely susceptible to “foot-in-the-door” prompt injection: once the agent’s thought commits to a particular tool, the likelihood of invoking that tool increases sharply even on poisoned evidence, allowing attackers to redirect the agent through indirect injection [88]. Section 8.2 returns to this attack family, consolidates the corresponding layered defenses (Table 4), and quantifies how rarely the multi-step failure modes that ReAct chains enable are evaluated in the surveyed corpus. 5.2.3. Plan-and-Solve Plan-and-solve agents produce a complete plan before any tool invocation, executing each step only after global feasibility has been confirmed. AutoPentest [32,35] and PenHeal [32] use plan-and-solve to enumerate attack stages before exploit selection. The pattern is more auditable than ReAct because the plan is inspectable, but it sacrifices adaptiveness: discoveries during execution rarely reshape the plan unless an explicit replanning step is included. 5.2.4. Reflection and Self-Critique Reflection inserts a critique pass between draft and final output. Polymorphic-prompt defenses against prompt injection [88] and the meta-cognitive judgment function for governable autonomy use reflection to gate decision commit. Reflection raises latency by 1.5–2× per task but is essential for high-stakes security decisions where false action is unrecoverable. 5.3. Pillar III—Action Scope Action scope concerns what the agent is empowered to do. 5.3.1. Detection-Only The agent classifies inputs as benign or malicious and reports findings to a human or downstream system. This is the dominant scope in the IoT-IDS subset of our corpus: of the 41 studies coded as primary IoT-IDS work, 38 (approximately 93%) operate in detectiononly mode, with representative examples spanning hybrid LLM-IDS architectures, edge transformers, and BERT-based federated detectors [16,17,65,71–74,85,113,114]. Detectiononly is the safest scope but does not realize the autonomy promise of agentic AI. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15122740 Electronics 2026, 15, 2740 16 of 42 5.3.2. Response Orchestration The agent selects and executes responses—blocking IPs, quarantining devices, trig- gering playbooks, generating tickets [28,115–117]. SOAR-LLM [28] dynamically generates and adapts response playbooks rather than following static templates; CyberAlly [27] is reported to have cut MTTR from 8 h to 90 min (vendor case-study figures, not an independent benchmark) in simulated SOC environments. Response-scope agents must be paired with strong governance controls because a hallucinated quarantine command can take down a hospital network as easily as a botnet command. 5.3.3. Threat Hunting Proactive search for adversary presence given hypotheses, MITRE ATT&CK tech- niques, or threat-intelligence indicators [24,25,87,129]. Threat-hunting agents are deeply ReAct-natured because hunts require iterative evidence gathering. Recent work integrates Splunk SOC pipelines with autonomous LLM agents that generate hunt scripts, execute them in sandboxes, learn from execution feedback, and adapt their models over time [25]. 5.3.4. Vulnerability Discovery Agents identify exploitable flaws in firmware, configurations, or smart contracts. CID4IoT [40] performs LLM-guided command-injection detection in IoT web services; LLM-powered protected-interface evasion frameworks discover broken access control in IoT firmware [120]; LLM-Boofuzz [109] orchestrates black-box protocol fuzzing with LLM-generated test cases against MQTT and CoAP stacks. Multi-agent smart-contract auditors [119,121–123,125] now match or exceed expert auditors on standard benchmarks. The autonomous vulnerability-discovery scope is the closest agentic AI has come to delivering genuine novel security knowledge. Compared to coverage-guided fuzzers such as AFL++ and libFuzzer—which excel at exploring branch space within a single binary but treat every input byte as opaque—LLM-driven discovery contributes three capabilities. First, semantic input generation: an LLM that has read the Modbus or MQTT specification can construct protocol-valid inputs that pass parsing layers and probe deeper handlers [109]. Second, narrative explanation of crash signatures: rather than emitting a raw stack trace, the agent can summarize the suspected root cause in human-readable form for the auditor [32,123]. Third, taint-aware prioritization: the agent can ingest the output of a static analyzer (e.g., LATTE or FITS [41]) and steer the fuzzer toward sinks an attacker can reach. The cost is brittleness against unfamiliar protocols and the well-documented risk of hallucinated “vulnerabilities” that do not reproduce, both of which require human-in-the-loop verification [123,124]. The hallucinated-vulnerability rate deserves a more critical treatment than the literature currently gives it, because it is arguably the single largest obstacle between laboratory demonstrations of agentic vulnerability discovery and production deployment. Of the thirteen agentic vulnerability-discovery studies coded in our corpus (eight single-agent and five multi-agent; Table 5; non-agentic baselines and feasibility/tool-only entries in the coded matrix are outside this count), only two report any quantitative evidence about findings that fail to reproduce under independent verification [123,124]; the remaining eleven report detection-style metrics (precision, recall, benchmark pass rates) that, by construction, cannot distinguish a genuinely missed vulnerability from a hallucinated finding that was silently discarded during evaluation. The gap matters operationally. Every hallucinated finding consumes human triage effort, and because agentic discovery systems emit candidate findings at machine speed, even a modest false-discovery rate can saturate the very analyst capacity the agent was deplo

